The diesel figures do not include the low-emission zone fee, which starts on 2028-01-01 and would make diesel more expensive still.

## Risks

- **Electric range in winter.** Our longest route is 140 miles a day. Real-world range is about 180 miles in summer but about 150 in winter, so the longest two routes would be tight in January.
- **Charging.** We can charge overnight at the depot, but only with 6 chargers, so every electric van has to share and the charging schedule matters.
- **Electric purchase price.** The $58,200 price depends on the $7,500 incentive. If the incentive changes before we buy, electric costs more.
- **Hybrid emissions.** Hybrids remove range worries but still produce tailpipe emissions.
- **Diesel fees.** Diesel vans pay the city low-emission zone fee of $12.50 a day from 2028-01-01, during the life of these vans.

## Recommendation

**Buy electric vans for the 12 shorter routes and hybrid vans for the 6 longest.**

This mixed fleet costs $1,609,800 over five years for all 18 vans, about $316,200 less than staying with diesel. It costs $54,600 more than an all-electric fleet, but it keeps hybrids on the longest routes, where winter range would be too tight for an electric van, and it avoids the low-emission zone fee entirely.
